// Positive: a `Copy` type satisfies a `!Drop` bound, concretely and through a
// `T: Copy` generic.
//
// NOTE ON WHAT THIS ASSERTS. RFC-0072 §2.3 frames this as an *implication* —
// `T: Copy` derives `T: !Drop`. Before `Copy`/`Drop` were real aspects, this fixture
// tested it by giving one type both and relying on the override; that exact program is
// now rejected by RFC-0071 §4's declaration-site mutual exclusion (stage5_neg_33).
//
// An earlier revision of this comment claimed the implication had therefore become
// structurally guaranteed and untestable. **That was wrong**, and an adversarial review
// disproved it (#302): §4 was enforced only for *concrete* impl targets, so two
// overlapping conditional impls — `extend<T: Copy> Foo<T>: Copy` and
// `extend<T: Display> Foo<T>: Drop` — gave `Foo<i64>` both, and the implication was
// what let it through a `!Drop` bound.
//
// That hole is closed: `coherence` now checks the two aspects against each other for
// open targets too. The implication is still not *structurally* guaranteed — it holds
// because two separate checks enforce §4, not because the type system makes the
// combination unrepresentable — so it stays worth testing. See
// `typechecking/structs/stage5_neg_34_copy_and_drop_overlapping_conditional_impls.mtl`
// for the case that used to slip through.
//
// What this fixture covers is the ordinary path only: a `Copy` type satisfies `!Drop`,
// concretely and through a `T: Copy` generic. It does not isolate the implication —
// `Both` has no `Drop` impl, so `!Drop` would hold regardless.

copy struct Both {
    x: i64,
}

fun needs_no_drop<T: !Drop>(x: T) -> T {
    return x;
}

// Forwarding through a `T: Copy` parameter: at instantiation `T` is `Both`, which
// cannot implement `Drop`, so the `!Drop` bound holds.
fun via_copy<T: Copy>(x: T) -> T {
    return needs_no_drop(x);
}

fun main() {
    let b := Both { x = 1 };
    let direct := needs_no_drop(b);
    assert(direct.x == 1);

    let through_generic := via_copy(b);
    assert(through_generic.x == 1);
}
